+# Shared expenses service
+
+A small HTTP service that records who paid for what in a group and works out who owes whom. It is one file, `server.js`, and uses only Node.js built-in modules (no `npm install`). It was tested on Node v26.12.1.
+
+## Running it
+
+```sh
+PORT=8080 DATA_FILE=/var/lib/expenses/ledger.json node server.js
+```
+
+- `PORT`: the port to listen on. Unset, it is 3000. `PORT=0` picks a free port.
+- `DATA_FILE`: the JSON file that holds every group and expense, so they survive a restart. Unset, it is `expenses.json` in the working directory. A missing file, an empty file or a file holding only whitespace starts with no groups. A file holding anything else that is not valid JSON stops the service at startup with an error (exit code 1).
+
+On start the service prints `Listening on port <n>`. Stop it with SIGTERM or Ctrl-C. Every accepted change is already on disk when its reply is sent.
+
+Run the tests with `node --test`.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+Requests and replies are JSON. Every error reply is an object `{"error": "<message>"}`. Amounts are whole cents.
+
+### POST /groups
+
+Request: `{"id": "<group id>", "members": ["<name>", ...]}`
+
+| Status | Reply | When |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 201 | `{"id": "<id>", "members": [...]}` | Created. `members` is the stored list (see duplicates below). |
+| 400 | `{"error":"Invalid group ID"}` | The body is not a JSON object, or `id` is not a string with some non-whitespace text, or `id` is `.` or `..`, or `id` holds a lone UTF-16 surrogate. No URL can address such an ID. |
+| 409 | `{"error":"Group already exists"}` | A group with this `id` exists. |
+| 400 | `{"error":"Invalid group members"}` | `members` is not an array, or an entry is not a string with some non-whitespace text. |
+| 400 | `{"error":"At least two distinct members are required"}` | Fewer than two distinct names. |
+
+### POST /groups/{id}/expenses
+
+Records an expense paid by one member. Two forms:
+
+- Equal split: `{"payer": "<name>", "cents": <n>, "split": "equal"}`. Each member owes `cents` divided by the number of members, rounded down. The cents left over go one each to the members listed first. For example, 100 among three members is 34, 33, 33.
+- Exact split: `{"payer": "<name>", "cents": <n>, "split": "exact", "shares": {"<name>": <cents>, ...}}`. Each listed member owes that many cents (zero is allowed). Members left out owe nothing. The shares must add up to `cents`.
+
+| Status | Reply | When |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 201 | `{"expense": <n>}` | Recorded. `n` is 1 for the group's first expense, then 2, and so on. A refused request does not use up a number. |
+| 404 | `{"error":"Group not found"}` | No such group. This is checked before the body. |
+| 400 | `{"error":"Invalid expense"}` | The body is not a JSON object, or `payer` is not a member, or `cents` is not a whole number from 1 to 9,007,199,254,740,991. |
+| 400 | `{"error":"Invalid split or shares"}` | `split` is not `equal` or `exact`. For `exact`: `shares` is not an object, names someone who is not a member, holds a share that is not a whole number of 0 or more, or does not add up to `cents`. |
+| 500 | `{"error":"Internal server error"}` | The data file could not be written. See below. |
+
+Numbers are judged by how they are written in the JSON: `100`, `100.0` and `1e2` are all the whole number 100 and are accepted. `1.5` is refused, and so is `1.0000000000000001`, which a JSON parser would otherwise round to 1. A number sent as a string, such as `"10"`, is refused.
+
+### GET /groups/{id}/balances
+
+200 with an object giving each member's net cents, in member order: what they paid minus what they owe. Positive means they are owed money. The values add up to 0. For example: `{"Ada": 50, "Bo": -50}`.
+
+404 `{"error":"Group not found"}` for an unknown group.
+
+### GET /groups/{id}/settlements
+
+200 with an array of payments `{"from": "<name>", "to": "<name>", "cents": <n>}` that bring every balance to 0. It is built step by step: the member who owes the most pays the member who is owed the most the smaller of the two amounts. Ties go to the member listed first in the group. When every balance is already 0 the array is empty: `[]`.
+
+404 `{"error":"Group not found"}` for an unknown group.
+
+### Anything else
+
+404 `{"error":"Route not found"}`. Under `/groups/{id}`, an unknown group answers 404 `{"error":"Group not found"}` on any path.
+
+## Behaviour you should know about
+
+These points were chosen by the builder, not by the request. Each was checked against the running service.
+
+- **Duplicate members.** Repeated names in `members` collapse to one member, kept at its first position. `["Bo","Ada","Bo"]` becomes `["Bo","Ada"]`, and the 201 reply shows that list.
+- **IDs and names.** Group IDs and member names must be strings with some non-whitespace text. They are matched exactly, with case and surrounding spaces kept: `ada` is not `Ada`, and `" Trip "` is a different group from `"Trip"`.
+- **URL format.** The group ID is one path segment, percent-decoded. Encode it the way JavaScript's `encodeURIComponent` does, so `shared / café?` becomes `shared%20%2F%20caf%C3%A9%3F`. A malformed escape such as `%E0%A4%A` returns 400 `{"error":"Invalid group ID encoding"}`. Query strings are ignored.
+- **Unlisted methods and paths.** Any other method or path returns 404, including a trailing slash (`/groups/trip/balances/`), `GET /groups` and `GET /groups/{id}`. No route lists, changes or deletes groups or expenses.
+- **Group creation reply.** A 201 returns the ID and the stored member list. The ID is checked first (400), then whether it exists (409), then the members. So an existing ID answers 409 even when its `members` are invalid.
+- **Request JSON.** No `Content-Type` header is needed. Properties the request does not use are ignored, for example `shares` on an equal split. If a property appears twice in one body, the last value is used.
+- **Error replies.** Every error is `{"error": "<message>"}`. If the data file cannot be written (for example, its directory was moved away), the request gets 500 `{"error":"Internal server error"}`, the file-system error is logged to stderr, and the change is not kept. Its expense number is not used. Once the directory is back, a retry succeeds.
+- **Amount range.** The largest single amount accepted is 9,007,199,254,740,991 cents. The next whole cent returns 400.
+- **Large totals.** Balances and settlement amounts are written as exact whole numbers, even above 9,007,199,254,740,991 (for example `18014398509482032`). A client that reads JSON numbers as doubles, as JavaScript's `JSON.parse` does, will round such amounts. Use a lossless JSON parser if totals can grow that large.
+- **Startup defaults.** If unset, `PORT` is 3000 and `DATA_FILE` is `expenses.json` in the working directory.
+- **Storage.** The data file is created by the first change that succeeds. A GET never creates it. Each change writes a new file beside the data file and renames it over the old one, so the file is replaced by a new file with owner-only read and write permissions (mode 0600). If `DATA_FILE` is a symbolic link, the link is kept and the file it points to is replaced, even if that file did not exist yet.
+- **One process per data file.** The file is read only at startup. Each change writes the whole ledger from the service's memory. Changes made to the file by anything else while the service runs are not seen, and the next change overwrites them. This includes a second copy of the service started on the same `DATA_FILE`: it is not detected or refused. Each copy keeps its own ledger, so expenses one copy accepted are lost when the other writes. The two copies can also hand out the same expense number. Stop the old process before you start its replacement. Within one process, simultaneous requests never lose an expense.
